Hey, all.  Been a while since my last Extropians post.  Life is good.
I've been out of the Extropian mainstream in the last year, since the
Xanadu venture disbanded.  But I'm being productive at work and very
expansive in my personal/emotional/social life, and despite a bout of
RSI, things are generally excellent.  In a couple years I will
probably tire of doing corporate multimedia-for-hire and get back into
agoric software or secure Webbery or something, but for now I just
want to get some relatively mundane but well-specified work done.

One comment on the superhero discussion:

Dan Davis wrote:
>By contrast, quite a lot of the bad guys are transhumanist. A lot of
>the extended stories are about some evil one trying to attain godhood.

Ray wrote:
>A good example of this is X-Men, particularly, the phoenix saga
>which aired last week. 
>The bad guy, who is
>trying to get the crystal, finally obtains it and starts the
>transformation of the galaxy into "a living galaxy" He will cause
>the death of a billion worlds in the process. It turns out,
>what the crystal does is change the entire galaxyinto a superbrain
>and uploads the user into it. In the final battle, the x-men
>(and the powerful extrarterrestials) are no match for the "blight"
>who rearranges matter at will.

[Spoiler alert for Vernor Vinge's book _A Fire Upon The Deep_]

Ding ding ding.  Sounds a lot like the Blight from _A Fire Upon The
Deep_.  Which, as I recall, was at one point considered a _very_
Extropian book... or was it?  After all, the primary threat in that
book was a transhumanist force that was consuming large portions of
the human-occupied galaxy, transforming it into something much
different--??more interesting? more developed??--than it had been
previously.  How different is that from the plot of the X-Men series?
And indeed, the way the heroes of _Fire_ conquered the Blight was
by plunging most of the galaxy into abysmal Slowness.  Not a very
Extropian maneuver, one would think.

So what are we to conclude?  That Vinge's heroes, and the X-Men,
should have embraced the Blight?  That over-rapid, nonconsensual
transformation is considered non-Extropian?  "If I can't upload, I'm
not coming to your galactic metamorphosis?"

Dr. Extropy, or How I Learned To Stop Worrying And Love The Blight?

Apologies if this was done to death a year ago and I missed it.
Rob

(p.s.  _Out of Control_ is indeed a great book.)


